From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 23:23:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09287/11362] Retire the skip-the-seed lever: refuted on 6
 paired cells

The anchoring story that looked like the sharpest finding of the session does
not survive its own test. Six paired cells, in-wave, same code state, same
pinned seeds. The broad trigger would have forfeited ar25 t2 outright -- 1.0 to
zero without its seed -- and that seed is 93% cell-right, so "visible mismatches
equal visible rows" was never a signal for noise. The narrow trigger splits its
two-cell population 1-1 with nothing measured separating the cells. The
mechanism claim is withdrawn: a seed's mismatch profile does not predict when
seeding hurts.

tu93 t0's win is real and fully out-of-sample (tail 1.0/1.0, scan clean), which
is exactly why it is recorded as a single-cell anomaly rather than a lever: a
genuine engine no statable trigger can reach.

Secondary finding worth more than the lever would have been: seeding is 2.6x
cheaper as well as better. 52.5 minutes against 134.1 across the same cells,
because blank-page runs grind the 12-turn cap where seeded runs converge by turn
seven. The shipped default now has a cost argument on top of the quality one.

Running the wave was still right. The comparison that motivated the lever was
cross-wave, and a load-bearing record claim should not be retired on a
confounded basis -- though ar25's trajectory reproduced step for step under the
same seeds, so that earlier evidence turns out to have been sound.

